= The Berlin Opposition

The Berlin-Brandenburg district is the biggest in the party, and since the summer it has been run by the Left. Ruth Fischer is twenty-five, Viennese, a brilliant speaker with a gift for contempt. Arkadi Maslow, born in Russia and educated in Germany, writes her theses. Together they have made Berlin into an opposition to the Zentrale.

Their complaint is the united front: it is a road back to the SPD, they say, and the leadership has lost its nerve. In the Berlin factory cells, where unemployment is rising, the argument goes down well.

- @co_opt: Bring Fischer into the Zentrale's work.
- @isolate: Isolate the Berlin leadership.
- @summon: Ask Moscow to call Maslow to Moscow.

@co_opt
subtitle: The Left's influence grows. Berlin calms down.
on-arrival: offensive_satisfaction += 10; district_berlin_loyalty += 10; unionist_satisfaction -= 4; lean_offensive += 1

Fischer is given responsibility for the party's work among the unemployed. She uses it to build her following across the country.

@isolate
subtitle: The Right's and the Spartacists' way. Berlin will resent it.
on-arrival: offensive_satisfaction -= 10; district_berlin_loyalty -= 10; unity -= 2; unionist_satisfaction += 4; luxemburgist_satisfaction += 4; lean_unionist += 1; lean_luxemburgist += 1

The Zentrale sends its own organisers to Berlin and builds a minority of the district around them. Every district meeting becomes a battle.

@summon
subtitle: [? if lock_moscow_orders = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] Moscow solves German problems by inviting them to Moscow.
choose-if: lock_moscow_orders < 2
